#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/platform.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm, generic parameter and
own-line parameter there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces
each on hover; the corpus runner ratchets that coverage under #~"make ci".
This page is the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit
is meant to be used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"platform" unit is what the compiler knows about the target it
is building for: its operating system and architecture, and the
triple that named them. It says nothing about the toolchain that
builds for it: which C compiler compiles the output, and how, are
the C backend's business. What a unit needs of that toolchain -- f128
needs #~"__float128" -- is in the C backend's table, which refuses a
program by name when its compiler lacks it.

Every member is a #b:"compile-time constant". A read is folded while
compiling, so it can drive a unit-level #~"if" or a #~"require:"
block, and a branch it decides never reaches the C.

Each is declared as a zero-argument #~"is native" function, and the
#~"out" type is the type. The type checker answers every one of them
itself, from the invocation it was given, and lowers it to a constant
of the checked program: no backend implements it and the emitted C
never names it. A member declared with a body is an error -- which is
what keeps these from being values that merely happen to agree with
the build.

#code language=zerolang: """
  require: unit {
      library: if platform.os == platform.oskind.windows then {
          "ws2_32"
      } else {
          ""
      }
  }
"""

Each kind is named for its #b:"values" rather than for the member
that reads it. That is what keeps #~"platform.platform" from ever
arising: the member is #~"os", its type is #~"oskind", and an arm is
#~"platform.oskind.linux".

#p: #b:"Members"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=os: #b:"platform.os"] -- #~"oskind"]
  [#*: [#> to=arch: #b:"platform.arch"] -- #~"archkind"]
  [#*: [#> to=target: #b:"platform.target"] -- #~"StringView"]
  [#*: [#> to=ptrbits: #b:"platform.ptrbits"] -- #~"u64"]

#p: #b:"Kinds"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=oskind: #b:"oskind"] -- #~"variant { linux: null  darwin: null  windows: null  other: null }"]
  [#*: [#> to=archkind: #b:"archkind"] -- #~"variant { x86_64: null  aarch64: null  other: null }"]


#--: oskind


Host operating-system family.


#--: archkind


CPU architecture family.


#--: os


The operating-system family being built for.

#code language=zerolang: """
  if platform.os.linux then { ... }
  if platform.os == platform.oskind.linux then { ... }
"""

Both spellings fold and agree: #~"value.arm" is always a predicate,
so the first asks whether the constant holds that arm.


#--: arch


The CPU architecture family being built for.


#--: target


The #~"--target" triple, or the empty string for a native build.


#--: ptrbits


The width of a pointer on the target, in bits: 64 on both
architectures #~"archkind" names. It is the width of a union's
payload slot, which decides whether a bare value-type arm is held
in the union itself or refused with the hint #~"(Box T)" -- see
the #~"union" section of the language spec.

#p: #b:"Caveat"

#~"os" and #~"arch" come from #~"--target" when one is given and from
the host otherwise. A cross #~"--cc" used #b:"without" #~"--target"
therefore leaves them describing the host while the C compiler
targets something else.

They are folded from a #b:"parse" of the triple, not from substring tests:
each #~"-" separated component is matched whole against the arm names and
their aliases, and a triple naming no operating system or two of them is
refused at the flag rather than folded. See the cross-compilation section
of #~"zc.pdoc" for the vocabulary.

#~"zl" and #~"zls" never see a #~"--target" — they resolve the triple as
empty — so a #~"require:" guard keyed on a cross target fires in
#~"zc build" and stays silent in the editor.
